\import{discrete-macros}

\title{#{\all{x}{D} P(x)}}
\date{2026-02-09T12:07:26Z}
\tag{discrete}
\tag{draft}

\p{To prove a proposition of the form #{\all{x}{D} P(x)}, suppose #{d} is an \em{arbitrary} element of #{D}, and prove #{P(d)}.  By \em{arbitrary}, we mean that we assume nothing about #{d} except that it is an element of the domain #{D}.  Note that you must make up a new (unused) variable name for #{d}!}

\transclude{005J}

\p{(Later we will see that for certain domains #{D} we can also use a \em{proof by induction}.)}

\p{If you know #{\all{x}{D} P(x)}, then you know #{P(d)} for any #{d} in the domain #{D}.}
